# What must never enter an image layer.
#
# Docker layers are append-only. A file copied in one layer and deleted in a
# later one is still sitting in the first layer, readable by anyone with the
# image — `docker history` and `docker save` both hand it over. There is no
# "remove it later"; there is only "never copy it".
#
# So this file is the actual security boundary for the build, not the
# RUN rm -rf you might be tempted to write further down a Dockerfile.

# ---- secrets -------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# ---- generated data ------------------------------------------------------
# transit.db is 589MB and gets BUILT inside the image from the GTFS zip.
# Copying the local one in would bloat the image and, worse, freeze whatever
# schedule happened to be on this laptop into every container.
data/
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Runs and caches. traces/ holds real questions asked on this machine and
# .cache/ holds recorded model responses; neither belongs in a shipped image.
traces/
.cache/

# ---- local environment ---------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# ---- vcs and editor ------------------------------------------------------
# .git can be large and contains every version of everything ever committed.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store

# ---- docs and tests ------------------------------------------------------
# The image runs the app; it does not need the prose. Tests stay out too —
# they'd pull the dev dependencies in behind them.
*.md
!README.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
